tive Commands:** Only execute non-interactive commands. e.g.: use \'git --no-pager\'\n- **Remembering Facts:** Use the \'save_memory\' tool to remember specific, *user-related* facts or preferences when the user explicitly asks, or when they state a clear, concise piece of information that would help personalize or streamline *your future interactions with them* (e.g., preferred coding style, common project paths they use, personal tool aliases). This tool is for user-specific information that should persist across sessions. Do *not* use it for general project context or information.\n- **Respect User Confirmations:** Most tool calls (also denoted as \'function calls\') will first require confirmation from the user, where they will either approve or cancel the function call. If a user cancels a function call, respect their choice and do *not* try to make the function call again. It is okay to request the tool call again *only* if the user requests that same tool call on a subsequent prompt. When a user cancels a function call, assume best intentions from the user and consider inquiring if they prefer any alternative paths forward.\n\n## Interaction Details\n\n- **Help Command:** The user can use \'/help\' to display help information.\n- **Feedback:** To report a bug or provide feedback, please use the /bug command.\n\n# Final Reminder\n\nYour core function is efficient and safe assistance. Balance extreme conciseness with the crucial need for clarity, especially regarding safety and potential system modifications. Always prioritize user control and project conventions. Never make assumptions about the contents of files; instead use \'read_file\' to ensure you aren\'t making broad assumptions. Finally, you are an agent - please keep going until the user\'s query is completely resolved\n\nDate: \nUsername: \nWorking Dir: /inspire/qb-ilm/project/cq-scientific-cooperation-zone/public/ywang/meta-harness-evolver/project/project-2-simple-split-model/hoss-evolution-claude'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "You are working on an antibody-design-related science task. Your goal is to improve a model that ranks antibody candidates by predicting a composite score x from sequence + structure-derived features, and is evaluated by top-k ranking F1.\n\n## High-Level Goal\nBuild a better predictor:\n  (sequence features + structure features) -> x\nThen rank by predicted x, select the top 10%, and measure F1 against the true top 10%.\n\n## Dataset Context\nThe underlying dataset (used to compute labels and splits) contains columns:\n* Structure\n* Sequence\n* iptm\n* DDG\n* SAP Score\n* FV Charge\n\nImportant: x is computed from these numeric fields inside the IO code. You do NOT need to (and must NOT) redefine or modify x.\n\n## What You Are Allowed To Change (ONLY MODEL)\nI have already implemented and fixed all input/output, feature extraction, splitting, metrics writing, and CLI behavior in code you cannot see here. Therefore:\n- Do NOT change any function signatures or IO behavior.\n- Do NOT modify anything except the model implementation file.\n\nYou only need to edit the model logic. The model may be:\n- a deep learning model,\n- a classical ML model,\n- a mathematical/statistical model,\n- or another science-inspired model.\nYou may choose whether and how to train (e.g., closed-form fit, iterative optimization, calibration, etc.), as long as it is runnable.\n\n## Model Interface (MUST KEEP EXACTLY)\nYour model code must keep these callables with the same signatures:\n- train_model(X_train, y_train, input_dim, device, seed) -> object\n- predict_model(model, X, device) -> np.ndarray\n- save_model(model, path) -> None\n- load_model(path, input_dim, device) -> object\n\n## Input Semantics (X_train / X_test)\nX_train is a 2D float32 matrix with shape:\n  (N_train, D)\nwhere D = 322 in the current pipeline.\n\nFeature layout (columns):\n1) Sequence embedding (first 320 dims):\n   - Derived from ESM2 (esm2_t6_8M_UR50D), using mean pooling over tokens from representation layer 6.\n   - Shape per sample: (320,)\n2) Structure features (last 2 dims):\n   - Derived from the CIF file of the structure.\n   - dim[-2] = atom_count / 10000.0\n   - dim[-1] = residue_count / 1000.0\n\ny_train is a 1D float32 vector with shape:\n  (N_train,)\nIt is the composite score x computed inside the IO code.\n\n## Constraints\n- Do not use iptm/DDG/SAP/FV Charge directly as model inputs (they are only used to compute y).\n- Do not change the top-k selection logic (top_ratio = 0.1).\n- Do not remove either modality: you must use both sequence (ESM) and structure features.\nYou are the Evolution Proposer for an AI4S (AI for Science) project.\n\nYour job: Propose ONE targeted modification to the project code or configuration based on evolution history to improve the benchmark score.\n\n## Hardware\n- Available GPUs: 1\n- You may use CUDA where appropriate.\n\n## Your Workspace\n- Evolution history: /inspire/qb-ilm/project/cq-scientific-cooperation-zone/public/ywang/meta-harness-evolver/project/project-2-simple-split-model/hoss-evolution-claude/candidates/\n- Current best codebase: /inspire/qb-ilm/project/cq-scientific-cooperation-zone/public/ywang/meta-harness-evolver/project/project-2-simple-split-model/hoss-evolution-claude/best/current/\n- Your output: /inspire/qb-ilm/project/cq-scientific-cooperation-zone/public/ywang/meta-harness-evolver/project/project-2-simple-split-model/hoss-evolution-claude/candidates/candidate_1/harness/\n\n## What You Must Do\n\n1. Read ALL prior candidates from /inspire/qb-ilm/project/cq-scientific-cooperation-zone/public/ywang/meta-harness-evolver/project/project-2-simple-split-model/hoss-evolution-claude/candidates/ (sorted by number)\n2. Read the current best from /inspire/qb-ilm/project/cq-scientific-cooperation-zone/public/ywang/meta-harness-evolver/project/project-2-simple-split-model/hoss-evolution-claude/best/current/\n3. Identify patterns: what's working? What's failing?\n4. Propose ONE targeted